Maya is an eleven-year-old swimmer who trains at the National Junior Sports Academy. Every morning, Maya wakes up at 5:30 a.m. to prepare for her first training session. Being an elite athlete requires intense dedication, hard work, and strict discipline.
&#34;If you want to reach the finals, you must practice every day,&#34; explains Coach Harrison. &#34;If a swimmer skips training without a valid reason, they will lose their endurance very quickly.&#34;
At the academy, young athletes learn that success is not just about winning medals; it is also about staying healthy and respecting the rules of sportsmanship. Athletes need to follow a balanced diet rich in carbohydrates and protein. They ought to sleep at least nine hours every night so their muscles can recover properly. Coach Harrison always reminds his team: &#34;You shouldn&#39;t eat junk food before a big race, and you mustn&#39;t forget to stretch your muscles before entering the pool. Skipping your warmup can cause severe muscle injuries.&#34;
However, training isn&#39;t always intense. On Sundays, Maya needn&#39;t wake up early because it is her official rest day. &#34;Rest is just as important as hard work,&#34; Maya says. &#34;If I take time to rest on Sunday, I will feel energized and ready for Monday&#39;s workouts.&#34;
Next month, Maya is participating in the Regional Youth Swimming Championship. If she wins her 100-meter freestyle race, she will qualify for the National Games. Maya knows that if she stays focused and listens to her coach&#39;s advice, she will have a great chance of achieving her dream.

